            steps: ["Recall that endogeneity arises when $\\text{Cov}(D, u) \\neq 0$, where D is treatment and u is the causal error. This violates the", "Under random assignment, treatment D is assigned independently of all pre-treatment variables and potential outcomes. This breaks any correlation between D and u.", "Formally, random assignment implies: $\\text{Cov}(D, u) = 0$ and more generally, $D \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp u$ (D is independent of the error)", "Under random assignment, the is satisfied: $E[u] = 0$ (unconditional on D) and $\\text{Cov}(D, u) = 0$", "Therefore, OLS of Y on D recovers $\\hat{\\beta}_1 = \\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_0$, which equals the causal treatment effect: $\\beta_1 = E[Y|D=1] - E[Y|D=0]$", "This resolves confounding: the control group (D=0) provides an unbiased counterfactual for what the treatment group (D=1) would have experienced absent treatment, because both groups are observationally equivalent before randomization."],
