            steps: ["Use the CLT distribution: $\\bar{X}_n \\approx N(\\mu, \\sigma^2/n)$ 2. Standardize by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard error: $$P(\\bar{X}_n > c) = P\\left(\\frac{\\bar{X}_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} > \\frac{c - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}}\\right)$$ 3. Define the standard normal variable: $Z = \\frac{\\bar{X}_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)$ 4. Rewrite: $P(\\bar{X}_n > c) = P\\left(Z > \\frac{c - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}}\\right)$ 5. Substitute numerical values for $c$, $\\mu$, $\\sigma$, and $n$, then calculate the z-score argument 6. Use standard normal cumulative distribution tables or the $\\Phi$ function: - $P(Z > z_0) = 1 - \\Phi(z_0)$ - $P(a < Z < b) = \\Phi(b) - \\Phi(a)$ 7. **Common mistake**: Forgetting to divide by $\\sqrt{n}$ (not $n$) when standardizing; using the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ instead of the standard error $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$"],
