            steps: ["For 95% CI: Critical value $c_{\\alpha/2} = 1.96$, Formula: $\\hat{\\beta}_j \\pm 1.96 \\cdot \\text{se}(\\hat{\\beta}_j)$ 2. For 99% CI: Critical value $2.576$, Formula: $\\hat{\\beta}_j \\pm 2.576 \\cdot \\text{se}(\\hat{\\beta}_j)$ 3. Correct interpretation: 95% of CIs from repeated sampling would contain true parameter 4. Common incorrect interpretations: 95% probability parameter is in interval, 95% of data in interval 5. Connection to testing: If 95% CI excludes $c$, reject $H_0: \\beta_j = c$ at 5% level 6. One-sided CIs: Lower bound: $[\\hat{\\beta}_j - 1.645 \\cdot \\text{se}, \\infty)$ 7. Common mistake: Using 1.96 for one-sided CI instead of 1.645"],
