            whatItAsks: "Test whether multiple coefficients are jointly zero - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS4 Q3#Part (I)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/F-test for Joint Hypotheses]]",
            steps: ["State hypotheses: $H_0: \\beta_j = ... = \\beta_k = 0$ vs $H_1$: at least one nonzero 2. Compute F-statistic: $$F^{\\text{act}} = \\frac{(SSR_r - SSR_u) / q}{SSR_u / (n - k - 1)}$$ 3. Alternatively using $R^2$: $$F^{\\text{act}} = \\frac{(R^2_u - R^2_r) / q}{(1 - R^2_u) / (n - k - 1)}$$ 4. Null distribution: $F^{\\text{act}} \\xrightarrow{d} F_{q, \\infty}$ 5. Critical value at 5%: For $q=1$: $3.84 \\approx 1.96^2$; for larger $q$ consult tables 6. Decision: Reject $H_0$ if $F^{\\text{act}} > F_{q,\\infty}^{0.05}$ 7. p-value: $p = 1 - F_{q,n-k-1}(F^{\\text{act}})$ 8. Interpretation: If $p < 0.05$, at least one tested coefficient is significantly different from zero 9. Common mistakes: Computing SSR ratio wrong, mixing up df, confusing with t-tests"],
