            whatItAsks: "Explain when to use one-sided tests. Compare power of one-sided vs two-sided. - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS1 Q14]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS4 Q2]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Hypothesis Testing]]",
            steps: ["Define one-sided vs two-sided: Two-sided: $H_0: \\beta_j = c$ vs $H_1: \\beta_j \\neq c$ | One-sided: $H_1: \\beta_j > c$ or $H_1: \\beta_j < c$ 2. When to use one-sided: Economic theory rules out one direction, must decide before seeing data 3. Critical values: Two-sided 5%: $1.96$ | One-sided 5%: $1.645$ 4. Power comparison: One-sided has higher power in nominated direction, zero power in opposite 5. Example: True $\\beta_1 = 0.5$, SE $= 0.2$ gives $t \\approx 2.5$; one-sided more powerful than two-sided 6. Trade-off: Higher power in one direction costs zero power in other; use one-sided only if theoretically justified 7. Common mistake: Choosing one-sided after seeing data, multiple one-sided tests without adjustment"],
