            steps: ["Set up: Sample 1 with mean $\\bar{Y}_1$, variance $s_1^2$; Sample 2 with mean $\\bar{Y}_2$, variance $s_2^2$; independent samples 2. State hypotheses: $H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2$ vs $H_1: \\mu_1 \\neq \\mu_2$ (or one-sided) 3. Pooled SE: $\\text{se}(\\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_2) = s_p\\sqrt{1/n_1 + 1/n_2}$ where $s_p^2 = [(n_1-1)s_1^2 + (n_2-1)s_2^2]/(n_1+n_2-2)$ 4. Unpooled SE (preferred): $\\text{se}(\\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_2) = \\sqrt{s_1^2/n_1 + s_2^2/n_2}$ 5. t-statistic: $t^{\\text{act}} = (\\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_2) / \\text{se}(\\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_2)$ 6. Null distribution: $t^{\\text{act}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)$ for large samples 7. Compare to critical value 1.96 (5%, two-sided) 8. Compute p-value: $p = 2[1 - \\Phi(|t^{\\text{act}}|)]$ 9. Confidence interval: $(\\bar{Y}_1 - \\bar{Y}_2) \\pm 1.96 \\cdot \\text{se}$ 10. Common mistakes: Using pooled SE when variances unequal, treating dependent samples as independent, forgetting $\\sqrt{2}$ factor"],
            concepts: ["Hypothesis Testing"],
