            whatItAsks: "Given regression output, compute $R^2$ and $\\bar{R}^2$. Explain why they differ. - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2022 Q5#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS3 Q7#Part (C)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS4 Q4#Part (A)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/R-squared and Adjusted R-squared]]",
            steps: ["Identify regression sums of squares: TSS = total variation, SSR = unexplained, ESS = explained, TSS = ESS + SSR 2. Compute $R^2 = 1 - SSR/TSS = ESS/TSS$ (ranges 0 to 1) 3. Compute Adjusted $R^2 = 1 - [SSR/(n-k-1)] / [TSS/(n-1)] = 1 - [(n-1)/(n-k-1)](1-R^2)$ 4. Explain difference: $R^2$ always increases with regressors; $\\bar{R}^2$ penalizes for extra regressors 5. When irrelevant variable added: $(1-R^2)$ decreases but $(n-1)/(n-k-1)$ increases, so $\\bar{R}^2$ may decrease 6. Interpretation: High $R^2$ (0.90) = strong fit; Low $R^2$ (0.15) = weak fit but still useful with stochastic factors 7. Common mistakes: High $R^2$ implies causality, using $R^2$ to compare different sample sizes, confusing with correlation"],
            concepts: ["R-squared and Adjusted R-squared"],
