            steps: ["Property of OLS: Adding any regressor weakly decreases SSR (OLS minimizes SSR with more flexibility) 2. Consequence for $R^2$: If SSR decreases, $R^2 = 1 - SSR/TSS$ increases, so $R^2$ is non-decreasing in number of regressors 3. Example with irrelevant variables: Even irrelevant $Z$ added to true model increases $R^2$ due to sampling variation 4. Low $R^2$ not necessarily bad: Cross-sectional data with heterogeneity naturally has low $R^2$ (many unmodeled factors) 5. High $R^2$ not sufficient: Can come from irrelevant variables or overfitting; out-of-sample $R^2$ more informative 6. Causality issue: High $R^2$ does not imply causality (e.g., ice cream sales and drowning both driven by summer) 7. Comparing models: Nested: use $\\bar{R}^2$ or F-test | Non-nested: use AIC/BIC 8. Common mistake: Assuming $R^2$ improvement means variable is important (need t-test or F-test)"],
