            whatItAsks: "If $Y^* = aY + c$ and $X^* = bX + d$, how do $\\hat{\\beta}_0, \\hat{\\beta}_1$, SER, and $R^2$ change? - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS3 Q7]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/OLS Estimators]], [[Concepts/R-squared and Adjusted R-squared]]",
            steps: ["Original regression: $Y_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_i + u_i$ 2. Transform: $Y_i^* = aY_i + c$, $X_i^* = bX_i + d$ 3. Derive: $Y_i^* = a\\beta_0 + c + a\\beta_1 X_i + au_i = (a\\beta_0 + c - a\\beta_1 d/b) + (a/b)\\beta_1 X_i^* + au_i$ 4. Transformed slope: $\\hat{\\beta}_1^* = (a/b)\\hat{\\beta}_1$ 5. SE transformation: $\\text{se}(\\hat{\\beta}_1^*) = |a/b| \\text{se}(\\hat{\\beta}_1)$ 6. SER transformation: $\\text{SER}^* = |a| \\cdot \\text{SER}$ (residuals scale by $a$) 7. $R^2$ transformation: $R^2^* = R^2$ (unchanged, scale-invariant) 8. Summary: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\to (a/b)\\hat{\\beta}_1$, SE $\\to |a/b|$ SE, SER $\\to |a|$ SER, $R^2$ unchanged 9. Common mistakes: Thinking $R^2$ changes with scaling, confusing $a/b$ vs $b/a$, forgetting shift terms"],
            concepts: ["OLS Estimators", "R-squared and Adjusted R-squared"],
