            steps: ["Define linearity in parameters: $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 Z_1 + ... + \\beta_k Z_k + u$ where $\\beta$'s appear linearly, regressors can be transformations 2. Verify polynomial $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + \\beta_2 X^2 + u$: Define $Z_1=X, Z_2=X^2$, rewrite as linear model, linear in parameters ✓ 3. Log model $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\log(X) + u$: Define $Z_1=\\log(X)$, linear in parameters ✓ 4. Nonlinear example $Y = \\beta_0 X^{\\beta_1} u$: Not linear in parameters ✗ 5. Orthogonality conditions: $E[u|Z_1,...,Z_k] = 0$, $\\text{Cov}(Z_j, u) = 0$, nonzero finite variance 6. Consequence: Under OR conditions, $\\hat{\\beta}_j$ unbiased, consistent, asymptotically normal 7. Common mistake: Confusing 'linear in parameters' with 'linear regression model' (nonlinear relationships like $X^2$ are still linear in parameters)"],
