            steps: ["Log-log model $\\log(Y) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\log(X) + u$: $\\beta_1$ is elasticity, 1% increase in $X \\to \\beta_1\\%$ increase in $Y$ 2. Log-linear $\\log(Y) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + u$: $\\beta_1$ is semi-elasticity, one-unit increase in $X \\to 100\\beta_1\\%$ increase in $Y$ 3. Linear-log $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\log(X) + u$: 1% increase in $X \\to \\beta_1/100$ unit increase in $Y$ 4. Cobb-Douglas $Y = AK^\\alpha L^\\beta e^u$: Log form $\\log(Y) = \\log(A) + \\alpha \\log(K) + \\beta \\log(L) + u$ 5. $\\alpha$ = elasticity w.r.t. capital, $\\beta$ = elasticity w.r.t. labor 6. Returns to scale: $\\alpha + \\beta <1$ (decreasing), $=1$ (constant), $>1$ (increasing) 7. When to use: Log-log for elasticities; log-linear for growth; linear-log for diminishing effects 8. CI for elasticity: In log-log, use $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\pm 1.96 \\cdot \\text{se}(\\hat{\\beta}_1)$ 9. Common mistakes: Misinterpreting log-linear coefficient (0.03 = 3 percentage points not 3%), confusing elasticity with marginal effect"],
