            id: "6.1",
            name: "Explain heteroskedasticity and its consequences",
            whatItAsks: "What is heteroskedasticity? Does it affect consistency of OLS? - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2021 Q6#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2021 Q6#Part (B)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2021 Q6#Part (C)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS4 Q1]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Heteroskedasticity]], [[Concepts/Standard Errors]]",
            steps: ["Define heteroskedasticity: Variance of error is not constant, $\\text{Var}(u_i | X_i) = \\sigma_i^2$ varies with $i$ (not constant $\\sigma^2$) 2. Example: Wage regression with higher variability for high-paid workers, firm profits increase with firm size 3. Effect on OLS consistency: OLS coefficients still consistent, $\\hat{\\beta}_j \\xrightarrow{p} \\beta_j$ (consistency depends only on orthogonality) 4. Effect on standard errors: Homoskedastic SE formula is WRONG under heteroskedasticity 5. Homoskedastic SE: $\\text{se}_{\\text{hom}} = \\sqrt{\\hat{\\sigma}^2 (X'X)_{jj}^{-1}}$ assumes $\\text{Var}(u_i|X_i) = \\sigma^2$ (false under heteroskedasticity) 6. Consequence: Confidence intervals, t-tests, p-values are incorrect 7. Robust SE (White's): $\\text{se}_{\\text{robust}} = \\sqrt{(X'X)^{-1} X' \\Omega X (X'X)^{-1}}$ where $\\Omega$ has diagonal $\\hat{u}_i^2$ 8. Key principle: Robust SEs always valid (both homoskedastic and heteroskedastic); homoskedastic SEs only valid under homoskedasticity 9. Common mistake: Confusing effect on point estimates (no effect) with effect on inference (serious)"],
            concepts: ["Heteroskedasticity", "Standard Errors"],
            appearedIn: "2021 Q6, 2021 Q6, 2021 Q6, WS4 Q1"
