            whatItAsks: "In $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + u$, explain why $\\text{Cov}(X, u) \\neq 0$ - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Recognize that the regression model has a structural error $u = Y - \\beta_0 - \\beta_1 X$ that captures all omitted factors 2. List potential sources of endogeneity: omitted variables, measurement error in X, or simultaneity ([[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]]) 3. For omitted variables: identify a variable $X_2$ that (a) affects Y ($\\text{Cov}(X_2, Y) \\neq 0$), and (b) is correlated with X ($\\text{Cov}(X, X_2) \\neq 0$). Name it specifically (e.g., \"ability\" in earnings models) 4. Show that $X_2$ is absorbed into $u$: $u = \\beta_2 X_2 + (\\text{other factors})$ 5. Therefore: $\\text{Cov}(X, u) = \\text{Cov}(X, \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots) = \\beta_2 \\text{Cov}(X, X_2) \\neq 0$ ([[Concepts/Omitted Variable Bias]]) 6. For measurement error: show that $X^* = X + e_X$ (observed with error) implies $\\text{Cov}(X^*, u - \\beta_1 e_X) \\neq 0$ ([[Concepts/Measurement Error and Attenuation Bias]]) 7. For simultaneity: demonstrate that X and Y are jointly determined (e.g., supply and demand), creating feedback where $u$ influences X ([[Concepts/Simultaneity Bias]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2020 Q7#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2021 Q5#Part (B)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2022 Q6#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2023 Q6#Part (B)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2024 Q6#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2025 Q6#Part (A)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]]",
