            name: "Determine direction of OVB",
            whatItAsks: "Is $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ biased upward or downward due to the omitted variable? - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Recall the OVB formula: $\\text{Bias}(\\hat{\\beta}_1^{OLS}) = \\beta_2 \\cdot \\text{Cov}(X_1, X_2)/\\text{Var}(X_1)$ ([[Concepts/Omitted Variable Bias]]) 2. Identify $\\beta_2$: the causal effect of omitted variable $X_2$ on Y in the true model. Determine its sign from economic theory 3. Calculate $\\text{Cov}(X_1, X_2)$: are the observed regressor and omitted variable positively or negatively correlated? Use economic reasoning 4. Apply the formula: sign of bias = sign($\\beta_2$) × sign($\\text{Cov}(X_1, X_2)$) 5. Interpret: if bias is positive, OLS estimate is biased upward (larger than true $\\beta_1$); if negative, biased downward 6. Verify with direction of correlation: if both $\\beta_2 > 0$ and $\\text{Cov}(X_1, X_2) > 0$, then $\\hat{\\beta}_1^{OLS}$ overstates the effect of $X_1$ 7. Check consistency: the magnitude of bias is $\\beta_2 \\cdot \\text{Corr}(X_1, X_2) \\cdot \\sigma_{X_2}/\\sigma_{X_1}$ ([[Concepts/Confounding Variables]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2023 Q5]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2025 Q1#Part (C)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS5 Q1#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS5 Q1#Part (B)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Omitted Variable Bias]], [[Concepts/Confounding Variables]]",
