            name: "Derive the large-sample limit of OLS under endogeneity",
            whatItAsks: "Show what $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ converges to when OR fails - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Start with the OLS estimator: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 = \\frac{\\sum (X_i - \\bar{X})(Y_i - \\bar{Y})}{\\sum (X_i - \\bar{X})^2}$ ([[Concepts/OLS Estimators]]) 2. Rewrite using sample covariance and variance: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 = \\frac{\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(X, Y)}{\\widehat{\\text{Var}}(X)}$ 3. Apply the Law of Large Numbers: $\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(X, Y) \\xrightarrow{p} \\text{Cov}(X, Y)$ and $\\widehat{\\text{Var}}(X) \\xrightarrow{p} \\text{Var}(X)$ ([[Concepts/Law of Large Numbers]]) 4. By the Continuous Mapping Theorem: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\xrightarrow{p} \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\text{Var}(X)}$ ([[Concepts/Convergence Concepts#Continuous Mapping Theorem|CMT]]) 5. Decompose $\\text{Cov}(X, Y)$: since $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + u$, we have $\\text{Cov}(X, Y) = \\beta_1 \\text{Var}(X) + \\text{Cov}(X, u)$ 6. Substitute: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\xrightarrow{p} \\frac{\\beta_1 \\text{Var}(X) + \\text{Cov}(X, u)}{\\text{Var}(X)} = \\beta_1 + \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, u)}{\\text{Var}(X)}$ 7. Conclude: when $\\text{Cov}(X, u) \\neq 0$ (endogeneity), $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ converges to $\\beta_1$ plus a bias term that does not vanish 8. Note: the bias term $\\text{Cov}(X, u)/\\text{Var}(X)$ is the population analog; it persists regardless of sample size ([[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2022 Q6#Part (B)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2023 Q6#Part (B)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2024 Q1#Part (C)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2024 Q1#Part (D)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS5 Q2]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]], [[Concepts/OLS Estimators]]",
            steps: ["Start with the OLS estimator: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 = \\frac{\\sum (X_i - \\bar{X})(Y_i - \\bar{Y})}{\\sum (X_i - \\bar{X})^2}$", "Rewrite using sample covariance and variance: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 = \\frac{\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(X, Y)}{\\widehat{\\text{Var}}(X)}$", "Apply the Law of Large Numbers: $\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(X, Y) \\xrightarrow{p} \\text{Cov}(X, Y)$ and $\\widehat{\\text{Var}}(X) \\xrightarrow{p} \\text{Var}(X)$", "By the Continuous Mapping Theorem: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\xrightarrow{p} \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\text{Var}(X)}$", "Decompose $\\text{Cov}(X, Y)$: since $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + u$, we have $\\text{Cov}(X, Y) = \\beta_1 \\text{Var}(X) + \\text{Cov}(X, u)$", "Substitute: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\xrightarrow{p} \\frac{\\beta_1 \\text{Var}(X) + \\text{Cov}(X, u)}{\\text{Var}(X)} = \\beta_1 + \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, u)}{\\text{Var}(X)}$", "Conclude: when $\\text{Cov}(X, u) \\neq 0$ (endogeneity), $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ converges to $\\beta_1$ plus a bias term that does not vanish", "Note: the bias term $\\text{Cov}(X, u)/\\text{Var}(X)$ is the population analog; it persists regardless of sample size - **Appeared in**: , , , , - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
