            id: "1.4",
            name: "Explain why endogeneity persists in large samples",
            whatItAsks: "Clarify that endogeneity is a structural problem, not a precision problem - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Define consistency: an estimator $\\hat{\\theta}$ is consistent if $\\hat{\\theta} \\xrightarrow{p} \\theta$ as $n \\to \\infty$ ([[Concepts/Consistency and Asymptotic Normality]]) 2. State the requirement for OLS consistency: exogeneity condition $E[u | X] = 0$, which ensures $\\text{Cov}(X, u) = 0$ ([[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]]) 3. Recognize that when $\\text{Cov}(X, u) \\neq 0$, the exogeneity condition fails structurally—it is determined by the data generating process and cannot be fixed by collecting more data 4. Show mathematically: $\\hat{\\beta}_1 \\xrightarrow{p} \\beta_1 + \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, u)}{\\text{Var}(X)} \\neq \\beta_1$. The limit is constant, not random 5. Explain the distinction: sampling error (precision) decreases with $n$; bias remains constant. Standard errors shrink, but the estimate converges to the wrong value 6. Illustrate: as $n \\to \\infty$, $\\hat{\\beta}_1$ becomes more precisely centered on the wrong target $\\beta_1 + \\text{Cov}(X, u)/\\text{Var}(X)$ 7. Conclude: endogeneity is a fundamental identification problem requiring external solutions (IV, proxy variables, research design) rather than more data ([[Concepts/Instrumental Variables]]) - **Appeared in**: Implicit in many questions - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Consistency and Asymptotic Normality]], [[Concepts/Endogeneity Definition and Sources]]",
