          {
            id: "7.3",
            name: "IV with measurement error",
            whatItAsks: "",
            steps: ["**Set Up the Measurement Error Problem**: a. True model: $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X + u$ where X is the true regressor b. Observed regressor: $X^* = X + e_X$ (classical measurement error) c. Observed model: $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X^* + (u - \\beta_1 e_X)$ d. OLS bias (attenuation): $\\hat{\\beta}_1^{OLS} \\xrightarrow{p} \\beta_1 \\lambda$ where $\\lambda = \\text{Var}(X)/[\\text{Var}(X) + \\text{Var}(e_X)] < 1$", "**Propose an Instrument Z**: a. The instrument Z must be: - Correlated with the true X (Z1 — relevance) - Uncorrelated with the measurement error $e_X$ (key for solving measurement error problem) - Uncorrelated with the structural error u (Z2 — exogeneity) b. Conditions: - Z1: $\\text{Cov}(Z, X) \\neq 0$ (Z is relevant for true X) - Z2a: $\\text{Cov}(Z, e_X) = 0$ (Z is uncorrelated with measurement error — crucial!) - Z2b: $\\text{Cov}(Z, u) = 0$ (Z is exogenous to the structural error)", "**Show IV Consistency**: a. Observed first stage: $X^* = \\pi_0 + \\pi_1 Z + v$ b. Note: $X^* = (X + e_X) = \\pi_0 + \\pi_1 Z + v$ c. For consistency, we need the true X (not the mismeasured $X^*$) to relate to Z: $X = X^* - e_X = \\pi_0 + \\pi_1 Z + (v - e_X)$ d. The residual $(v - e_X)$ is still uncorrelated with Z by Z2a: $E[Z(v - e_X)] = E[Zv] - E[Ze_X] = 0 - 0 = 0$ e. Observed reduced form: $Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X^* + (u - \\beta_1 e_X) = \\gamma_0 + \\gamma_1 Z + \\varepsilon$ f. Decompose $\\gamma_1$: $\\text{Cov}(Y, Z) = \\text{Cov}(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X^* + u - \\beta_1 e_X, Z)$ $= \\beta_1 \\text{Cov}(X^*, Z) + \\text{Cov}(u, Z) - \\beta_1 \\text{Cov}(e_X, Z)$ $= \\beta_1 \\text{Cov}(X + e_X, Z) + 0 - \\beta_1 \\cdot 0$ (by Z2b and Z2a) $= \\beta_1 \\text{Cov}(X, Z)$ g. Calculate $\\text{Cov}(X^*, Z)$: $\\text{Cov}(X^*, Z) = \\text{Cov}(X + e_X, Z) = \\text{Cov}(X, Z) + \\text{Cov}(e_X, Z) = \\text{Cov}(X, Z)$ (by Z2a) h. IV estimator: $\\hat{\\beta}_1^{IV} = \\frac{\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(Y, Z)}{\\widehat{\\text{Cov}}(X^*, Z)} \\xrightarrow{p} \\frac{\\text{Cov}(Y, Z)}{\\text{Cov}(X^*, Z)} = \\frac{\\beta_1 \\text{Cov}(X, Z)}{\\text{Cov}(X, Z)} = \\beta_1$ i. **Conclusion**: IV is consistent ($\\hat{\\beta}_1^{IV} \\xrightarrow{p} \\beta_1$) despite measurement error in X", "**Key Condition: Z Uncorrelated with Measurement Error**: a. Z2a ($\\text{Cov}(Z, e_X) = 0$) is **essential** for IV to work under measurement error b. Why? If $\\text{Cov}(Z, e_X) \\neq 0$, then $\\text{Cov}(Z, X^*) \\neq \\text{Cov}(Z, X)$, and IV fails to isolate the true effect c. This condition cannot be tested directly (measurement error is unobserved) and must be justified on institutional grounds", "**Intuition**: a. Measurement error biases OLS toward zero because observed X is a noisy proxy for true X b. IV can correct this if the instrument is correlated with true X but not with the noise $e_X$ c. IV uses the exogenous variation in the instrument to identify the true relationship, bypassing the measurement noise", "**Comparison**: a. **OLS under measurement error**: biased downward (attenuation), no remedy if measurement error is present b. **IV under measurement error**: consistent if Z satisfies Z1, Z2a, and Z2b c. **Trade-off**: IV requires finding an instrument uncorrelated with the noise; OLS is simple but biased", "**Example: Earnings and Mismeasured Education**: a. True model: log(Earnings) = education + ability b. Education is mismeasured (recall error in surveys) c. Proposed instrument: years of education according to school records (administrative data) d. Z1: School records correlate with true education (high) e. Z2a: School records are not correlated with recall measurement error (measurement error is random) f. Z2b: School records are exogenous (assigned by age cutoff) g. Conclusion: IV using school records as instrument can consistently estimate the return to true education - **Appeared in**: - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["Measurement Error and Attenuation Bias", "Instrumental Variables"],
            appearedIn: "2024 Q4"
          }
