          {
            id: "1.3",
            name: "Derive properties of AR(2) processes",
            whatItAsks: "For $Y_t = \\beta_0 + \\rho_1 Y_{t-1} + \\rho_2 Y_{t-2} + \\varepsilon_t$, find mean, autocovariances, stationarity conditions - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. **Mean**: Under stationarity, $E[Y_t] = E[Y_{t-1}] = E[Y_{t-2}] = \\mu$. Take expectations: $$\\mu = \\beta_0 + \\rho_1 \\mu + \\rho_2 \\mu \\implies \\mu = \\frac{\\beta_0}{1 - \\rho_1 - \\rho_2}$$ (requires $\\rho_1 + \\rho_2 \\neq 1$) 2. **Autocovariances via Yule-Walker**: Center the process: $Y_t - \\mu = \\rho_1(Y_{t-1}-\\mu) + \\rho_2(Y_{t-2}-\\mu) + \\varepsilon_t$. For $h=0$: $$\\gamma_0 = \\rho_1 \\gamma_1 + \\rho_2 \\gamma_2 + \\sigma^2$$ For $h=1$, multiply by $(Y_{t-1}-\\mu)$ and take expectations: $$\\gamma_1 = \\rho_1 \\gamma_0 + \\rho_2 \\gamma_1$$ Solve: $\\gamma_1 = \\frac{\\rho_1}{1-\\rho_2} \\gamma_0$, giving ACF: $\\rho_1^{acf} = \\frac{\\rho_1}{1-\\rho_2}$ 3. For $h \\geq 2$, use the recursive relation: $$\\gamma_h = \\rho_1 \\gamma_{h-1} + \\rho_2 \\gamma_{h-2}$$ 4. **Stationarity**: Write characteristic equation $1 - \\rho_1 z - \\rho_2 z^2 = 0$. For stationarity, both roots must lie outside the unit circle (equivalently: $|\\rho_i| < 1$ for roots $\\rho_i$, or: $\\rho_1 + \\rho_2 < 1$, $\\rho_2 - \\rho_1 < 1$, $|\\rho_2| < 1$) ([[Concepts/AR Processes]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2023 Q2#Part (A)]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/2024 Q2]], [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS8 Q3]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/AR Processes]], [[Concepts/Autocovariance and Autocorrelation]]",
            steps: ["**Mean**: Under stationarity, $E[Y_t] = E[Y_{t-1}] = E[Y_{t-2}] = \\mu$. Take expectations: $$\\mu = \\beta_0 + \\rho_1 \\mu + \\rho_2 \\mu \\implies \\mu = \\frac{\\beta_0}{1 - \\rho_1 - \\rho_2}$$ (requires $\\rho_1 + \\rho_2 \\neq 1$)", "**Autocovariances via Yule-Walker**: Center the process: $Y_t - \\mu = \\rho_1(Y_{t-1}-\\mu) + \\rho_2(Y_{t-2}-\\mu) + \\varepsilon_t$. For $h=0$: $$\\gamma_0 = \\rho_1 \\gamma_1 + \\rho_2 \\gamma_2 + \\sigma^2$$ For $h=1$, multiply by $(Y_{t-1}-\\mu)$ and take expectations: $$\\gamma_1 = \\rho_1 \\gamma_0 + \\rho_2 \\gamma_1$$ Solve: $\\gamma_1 = \\frac{\\rho_1}{1-\\rho_2} \\gamma_0$, giving ACF: $\\rho_1^{acf} = \\frac{\\rho_1}{1-\\rho_2}$", "For $h \\geq 2$, use the recursive relation: $$\\gamma_h = \\rho_1 \\gamma_{h-1} + \\rho_2 \\gamma_{h-2}$$", "**Stationarity**: Write characteristic equation $1 - \\rho_1 z - \\rho_2 z^2 = 0$. For stationarity, both roots must lie outside the unit circle (equivalently: $|\\rho_i| < 1$ for roots $\\rho_i$, or: $\\rho_1 + \\rho_2 < 1$, $\\rho_2 - \\rho_1 < 1$, $|\\rho_2| < 1$) - **Appeared in**: , , - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["AR Processes", "Autocovariance and Autocorrelation"],
            appearedIn: "2023 Q2, 2024 Q2, WS8 Q3"
          },
          {
            id: "1.4",
            name: "Match ACF patterns to AR models",
            whatItAsks: "Given a correlogram (ACF plot), determine which AR model generated it - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Recall key patterns ([[Concepts/Autocovariance and Autocorrelation]]): - **AR(1) with $\\rho > 0$**: ACF decays exponentially from positive. All $\\rho_h = \\rho^h > 0$, starting at $\\rho_1 = \\rho$. - **AR(1) with $\\rho < 0$**: ACF alternates in sign (oscillates), decaying exponentially. $\\rho_h = \\rho^h$ alternates. - **AR(2)**: More flexible. Can produce damped oscillations (complex roots) or monotonic decay (real roots). 2. For complex roots in AR(2): Period of oscillation is $2\\pi / \\arg(z)$ where $z$ is the complex root. 3. For real roots: Monotonic decay, similar to AR(1). 4. **Matching procedure**: Look at the ACF plot. Count oscillations, determine sign pattern, and estimate decay rate to identify the AR order and parameters ([[Concepts/AR Processes]]). - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2024 Q2#Part (A)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Autocovariance and Autocorrelation]], [[Concepts/AR Processes]]",
            steps: ["Recall key patterns: - **AR(1) with $\\rho > 0$**: ACF decays exponentially from positive. All $\\rho_h = \\rho^h > 0$, starting at $\\rho_1 = \\rho$. - **AR(1) with $\\rho < 0$**: ACF alternates in sign (oscillates), decaying exponentially. $\\rho_h = \\rho^h$ alternates. - **AR(2)**: More flexible. Can produce damped oscillations (complex roots) or monotonic decay (real roots).", "For complex roots in AR(2): Period of oscillation is $2\\pi / \\arg(z)$ where $z$ is the complex root.", "For real roots: Monotonic decay, similar to AR(1).", "**Matching procedure**: Look at the ACF plot. Count oscillations, determine sign pattern, and estimate decay rate to identify the AR order and parameters. - **Appeared in**: - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["Autocovariance and Autocorrelation", "AR Processes"],
            appearedIn: "2024 Q2"
          },
          {
            id: "1.5",
            name: "Properties when $\\rho = 1$ (unit root case)",
            whatItAsks: "What happens when $\\rho = 1$? Derive mean, variance, stationarity. - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Start with $Y_t = \\beta_0 + Y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$ (random walk with drift when $\\beta_0 \\neq 0$) 2. **Recursively substitute** backwards: $Y_t = Y_0 + \\beta_0 \\cdot 1 + \\varepsilon_t + Y_{t-2} = Y_0 + \\beta_0 \\cdot 2 + \\varepsilon_t + \\varepsilon_{t-1} + Y_{t-2}$, and so on: $$Y_t = Y_0 + \\beta_0 t + \\sum_{s=1}^t \\varepsilon_s$$ 3. **Mean**: $E[Y_t] = Y_0 + \\beta_0 t$ (depends on $t$ → NOT constant) ([[Concepts/Unit Roots and Random Walks]]) 4. **Variance**: $$\\text{Var}(Y_t) = \\text{Var}\\left(\\sum_{s=1}^t \\varepsilon_s\\right) = t \\sigma^2$$ (grows without bound as $t \\to \\infty$ → NOT constant, variance is infinite) 5. **Autocovariance**: $\\text{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h})$ depends on $t$ (violates condition 3) 6. **Conclusion**: NOT weakly stationary. The process has a **stochastic trend** (permanent shocks) ([[Concepts/Stationarity]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2021 Q2]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/Unit Roots and Random Walks]], [[Concepts/Stationarity]]",
            steps: ["Start with $Y_t = \\beta_0 + Y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$ (random walk with drift when $\\beta_0 \\neq 0$)", "**Recursively substitute** backwards: $Y_t = Y_0 + \\beta_0 \\cdot 1 + \\varepsilon_t + Y_{t-2} = Y_0 + \\beta_0 \\cdot 2 + \\varepsilon_t + \\varepsilon_{t-1} + Y_{t-2}$, and so on: $$Y_t = Y_0 + \\beta_0 t + \\sum_{s=1}^t \\varepsilon_s$$", "**Mean**: $E[Y_t] = Y_0 + \\beta_0 t$ (depends on $t$ → NOT constant)", "**Variance**: $$\\text{Var}(Y_t) = \\text{Var}\\left(\\sum_{s=1}^t \\varepsilon_s\\right) = t \\sigma^2$$ (grows without bound as $t \\to \\infty$ → NOT constant, variance is infinite)", "**Autocovariance**: $\\text{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h})$ depends on $t$ (violates condition 3)", "**Conclusion**: NOT weakly stationary. The process has a **stochastic trend** (permanent shocks) - **Appeared in**: - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["Unit Roots and Random Walks", "Stationarity"],
            appearedIn: "2021 Q2"
          },
          {
            id: "1.6",
            name: "Discuss persistence",
            whatItAsks: "How persistent is the process? What does $\\rho$ close to 1 imply? - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. **Persistence intuition**: A shock $\\varepsilon_s$ in period $s$ affects $Y_{s+1}, Y_{s+2}, \\ldots$. Impact decays by factor $\\rho$ each period ([[Concepts/AR Processes]]) 2. **Effect of shock**: From Sub-question 1.1, shock $\\varepsilon_s$ contributes $\\rho^j \\varepsilon_s$ to $Y_{s+j}$ (in deviation from mean) 3. **Cumulative impact**: Total long-run effect is $\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty \\rho^j \\varepsilon_s = \\frac{1}{1-\\rho} \\varepsilon_s$ (for $|\\rho| < 1$) 4. **Half-life**: Time for shock to decay to 50% of original impact: $\\rho^{h^*} = 0.5 \\implies h^* = \\frac{\\ln(0.5)}{\\ln(|\\rho|)}$ - If $\\rho = 0.9$: $h^* = \\frac{\\ln(0.5)}{\\ln(0.9)} \\approx 6.6$ periods - If $\\rho = 0.99$: $h^* \\approx 69$ periods (very persistent) 5. **When $\\rho = 1$**: Cumulative impact is infinite ($\\sum \\rho^j = \\infty$). Shocks are **permanent** → infinite persistence ([[Concepts/Unit Roots and Random Walks]]) 6. Higher $|\\rho|$ → more persistent → shocks take longer to die out - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/2025 Q2#Part (D)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/AR Processes]], [[Concepts/Unit Roots and Random Walks]]",
            steps: ["**Persistence intuition**: A shock $\\varepsilon_s$ in period $s$ affects $Y_{s+1}, Y_{s+2}, \\ldots$. Impact decays by factor $\\rho$ each period", "**Effect of shock**: From Sub-question 1.1, shock $\\varepsilon_s$ contributes $\\rho^j \\varepsilon_s$ to $Y_{s+j}$ (in deviation from mean)", "**Cumulative impact**: Total long-run effect is $\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty \\rho^j \\varepsilon_s = \\frac{1}{1-\\rho} \\varepsilon_s$ (for $|\\rho| < 1$)", "**Half-life**: Time for shock to decay to 50% of original impact: $\\rho^{h^*} = 0.5 \\implies h^* = \\frac{\\ln(0.5)}{\\ln(|\\rho|)}$ - If $\\rho = 0.9$: $h^* = \\frac{\\ln(0.5)}{\\ln(0.9)} \\approx 6.6$ periods - If $\\rho = 0.99$: $h^* \\approx 69$ periods (very persistent)", "**When $\\rho = 1$**: Cumulative impact is infinite ($\\sum \\rho^j = \\infty$). Shocks are **permanent** → infinite persistence", "Higher $|\\rho|$ → more persistent → shocks take longer to die out - **Appeared in**: - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["AR Processes", "Unit Roots and Random Walks"],
            appearedIn: "2025 Q2"
          },
          {
            id: "1.7",
            name: "Derive AR(1) by repeated substitution",
            whatItAsks: "Express $Y_{s+t}$ in terms of $Y_s$ and past shocks - **Step-by-step guide**: 1. Start with AR(1): $Y_\\tau = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_{\\tau-1} + \\varepsilon_\\tau$ for any time $\\tau$ ([[Concepts/AR Processes]]) 2. **Substitute recursively**: For period $s+1$: $$Y_{s+1} = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_s + \\varepsilon_{s+1}$$ For period $s+2$: $$Y_{s+2} = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_{s+1} = \\beta_0 + \\rho(\\beta_0 + \\rho Y_s + \\varepsilon_{s+1}) = \\beta_0(1+\\rho) + \\rho^2 Y_s + \\rho \\varepsilon_{s+1} + \\varepsilon_{s+2}$$ For period $s+t$, continue this pattern: 3. **General formula**: $$Y_{s+t} = \\beta_0(1 + \\rho + \\rho^2 + \\cdots + \\rho^{t-1}) + \\rho^t Y_s + \\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j \\varepsilon_{s+t-j}$$ 4. **Simplify the constant term** (geometric series): $$\\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j = \\frac{1-\\rho^t}{1-\\rho}$$ (if $\\rho \\neq 1$) 5. **Final result**: $$Y_{s+t} = \\frac{\\beta_0(1-\\rho^t)}{1-\\rho} + \\rho^t Y_s + \\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j \\varepsilon_{s+t-j}$$ 6. **Interpretation**: Current value is a weighted average of the initial condition $Y_s$ (weight $\\rho^t \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$), the accumulated drift, and accumulated shocks (with exponentially decaying weights) ([[Concepts/AR Processes]], [[Concepts/Stationarity]]) - **Appeared in**: [[Cleaned Past Questions/WS7 Q1#Part (A)]] - **Relevant concepts**: [[Concepts/AR Processes]], [[Concepts/Stationarity]]",
            steps: ["Start with AR(1): $Y_\\tau = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_{\\tau-1} + \\varepsilon_\\tau$ for any time $\\tau$", "**Substitute recursively**: For period $s+1$: $$Y_{s+1} = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_s + \\varepsilon_{s+1}$$ For period $s+2$: $$Y_{s+2} = \\beta_0 + \\rho Y_{s+1} = \\beta_0 + \\rho(\\beta_0 + \\rho Y_s + \\varepsilon_{s+1}) = \\beta_0(1+\\rho) + \\rho^2 Y_s + \\rho \\varepsilon_{s+1} + \\varepsilon_{s+2}$$ For period $s+t$, continue this pattern:", "**General formula**: $$Y_{s+t} = \\beta_0(1 + \\rho + \\rho^2 + \\cdots + \\rho^{t-1}) + \\rho^t Y_s + \\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j \\varepsilon_{s+t-j}$$", "**Simplify the constant term** (geometric series): $$\\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j = \\frac{1-\\rho^t}{1-\\rho}$$ (if $\\rho \\neq 1$)", "**Final result**: $$Y_{s+t} = \\frac{\\beta_0(1-\\rho^t)}{1-\\rho} + \\rho^t Y_s + \\sum_{j=0}^{t-1} \\rho^j \\varepsilon_{s+t-j}$$", "**Interpretation**: Current value is a weighted average of the initial condition $Y_s$ (weight $\\rho^t \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$), the accumulated drift, and accumulated shocks (with exponentially decaying weights) (, ) - **Appeared in**: - **Relevant concepts**: ,"],
            concepts: ["AR Processes", "Stationarity"],
            appearedIn: "WS7 Q1"
          }
